import{_ as i,P as t,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.09 Love, Care, Recognition, Meaning, and Quality of Life","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.09 Love, Care, Recognition, Meaning, and Quality of Life","xkChars":0,"xkWords":489,"xkLessons":{"D.09":{"title":"D.09 Love, Care, Recognition, Meaning, and Quality of Life","lead":"Someone completing a task for you can indeed be helpful; whether it reduces burdens over time also depends on who notices needs, organizes, reminds, and checks. Love and care are more than a task list: companionship, quiet, rest, and mourning can have meaning in their own right.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 5108–5123; original anchor section-d-09; short quotation, translated from the Chinese: “Replaceability of labor does not mean replaceability of the loved person, and reducing tasks need not eliminate loss.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","This section separates love, care, and recognition into related but nonidentical questions. Love can be understood as experience, relationship, action, and norm; care involves noticing needs, undertaking responsibility, providing care, and responding; recognition concerns standing, respect, and shared life. Compressing these into gains in system function would omit experience itself, irreplaceable positions in interpersonal relationships, and normative questions about how people treat one another.","Private companionship, quiet observation, rest, hope, and mourning need not first demonstrate detectable change in a group’s structure to have meaning. The framework’s empirical thresholds limit certain specific knowledge claims; they should not become a universal admission ticket for life’s value. A claim that an activity repaired institutional feedback of course requires corresponding evidence; without that evidence, however, the person’s companionship, quiet, or remembrance is not thereby valueless.","Voluntariness, fairness, capacity, and outcomes require separate evidence. Willingness to help does not establish fair distribution of burdens; great sacrifice does not establish safe refusal; low cost does not establish absence of goodwill. Capacity answers whether one can undertake something, while outcomes answer what happened; sincere willingness cannot replace checks of capacity or consequences, nor can one unmet expectation erase the action and its evaluability.","HV11 describes particular bounded undertakings and their conditional consequences; it is not a total definition of love, still less a scale on which higher costs mean better personality. Use it within its applicable tasks and specific conditions, without forcing everyday goodwill, companionship, and relational experience into it. Actual undertaking under coercion may produce effects, but effects still cannot retrospectively establish free consent; established bounded undertaking does not authorize others to demand unlimited further sacrifice.","Death, separation, the ending of a relationship, and organizational exit involve different objects and clocks. Legal relationships, actual contact, living arrangements, emotional bonds, and mourning may change at different times; organizational X0 belongs only to its specified object and cannot be recast as a person’s death or a universal stage of grief. No clinical grief stages are offered here; the reminder is to preserve each process’s own questions and time in records.","Hope can support action, but wishes, probability judgments, plans, and revisable commitments are not the same kind of statement. Hoping for reunion does not mean reunion is highly probable; having a wish does not mean a feasible plan has been arranged; a commitment also requires stating one’s capacity for control, conditions, and revisable boundaries. Honesty about the future need not cancel hope, and hope must not manufacture a guaranteed future for others.","Exiting a relationship may require ending direct contact while children or other dependents still need safe continuity of care. Continuity must not force victims to remain exposed; a justified exit also does not automatically settle every bounded obligation with independent grounds. Actual arrangements depend on specific safety and professional conditions; this lesson provides only principled boundaries, not case-specific legal advice. Conceptual positions such as independent channels and suitable successors to care can be compared, but no real person can be assigned responsibility without facts and authorization.","Care work is not only execution: noticing needs, arranging schedules, reminding, checking, and being on call may also continuously consume attention and time. Completion of one task can be real help; a further claim of reducing someone’s burden over time requires checking whether coordination remains concentrated on that person and whether managing helpers adds work. Bounded help cannot be called no help at all, nor can it automatically be expanded into achieved fair sharing.","Replaceability of tasks and irreplaceability of the relationship’s person are not contradictory. A new caregiver may take over shopping or arranging travel, but this does not mean the loved person can be replaced on equivalent terms; fewer tasks do not guarantee less loss either. Relational meaning, actual workload, and emotional changes are different dimensions, so filling one service gap cannot establish that relationship loss has been resolved.","Happiness, dignity, freedom from harm, and meaning may be assessed alongside one another, without forcing the person to accept an all-determining total score chosen by the analyst. If a composite indicator is adopted for a bounded decision, disclose weights and non-offsettable requirements; a high happiness score cannot offset unauthorized harm or deprivation. This section focuses on preventing indicators from replacing people’s lives, not on denying all actual comparison.","The nine entry points are, in order: love as experience, relationship, and action; care ethics including Gilligan and Tronto; recognition traditions including Hegel and Honneth; discussions of meaning including Kierkegaard, Sartre, Camus, and Frankl; death, mourning, memory, and endings; sacrifice, gifts, dedication, and depletion; hope, faith, commitments, and the future; loneliness, belonging, friendship, and intimacy; and suffering, happiness, desire, and quality of life. The directory indicates the breadth of questions; it does not authorize erasing the thinkers’ differences into one mechanism. The strongest opposing position worries that separating components dilutes sincerity; the response is that clarifying conditions can protect real care while reducing demands for limitless sacrifice in its name."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"One act of help","leftText":"A task or need is actually taken up.","rightTitle":"Long-term burden reduction and fairness","rightText":"Still require checks of coordination, being on call, distribution, and continuing consequences."},{"leftTitle":"Willingness to give","leftText":"Expression or action shows willingness to help.","rightTitle":"Safe refusal and fairness","rightText":"High cost does not establish freedom, and willingness does not establish fair distribution."},{"leftTitle":"Replaceable tasks","leftText":"Other people can perform certain work.","rightTitle":"Replaceable person in a relationship","rightText":"Taking over work cannot establish that the loved person can be replaced."},{"leftTitle":"Hope and wishes","leftText":"Expectations or things valued about the future.","rightTitle":"Probability, plans, and commitments","rightText":"Each has its own evidence, capacity, arrangements, and revision conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Organizational exit X0","leftText":"An organizational-state marker for the applicable object.","rightTitle":"Death and mourning","rightText":"Objects and clocks differ; they cannot be imposed as universal life stages."}],"boundaries":["Do not treat HV11 as a definition of all love or goodwill, or rank personalities by cost.","Do not apply organizational X0 to individual death, loss, or stages of grief, or make clinical diagnoses.","Safe continuity of care cannot justify forcing victims to remain exposed; specific obligations require independent grounds.","Do not use this text to adjudicate exit, contact, or legal obligations in real relationships.","The real source is limited to conceptual statements in the author’s public research introduction; its summary is not used to reconstruct interview cases or full-text statistics."],"questions":["Why cannot long-term burden reduction be assessed only by counting task execution?","Does low-cost help necessarily lack goodwill?","Why should hope and probability judgments be separated?","Why does someone taking over tasks not guarantee the disappearance of loss?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-09/figures/section-d-09.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The author’s firsthand research introduction: bringing cognitive labor into household labor","href":"https://allison-daminger.squarespace.com/research"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The author’s firsthand research introduction: bringing cognitive labor into household labor","text":"In the first paragraph of “The Cognitive Dimension of Household Labor” on her own Research page, sociologist Allison Daminger explicitly identifies anticipating needs, identifying viable options, making decisions, and monitoring outcomes as cognitive labor. Her public account provides an actual research entry point for checking coordination work; this lesson uses it to pose checking questions, without reconstructing any details of couple interviews from the page or inferring division-of-labor proportions for all families.","sourceLabel":"Allison Daminger’s own Research page, version currently read","sourceUrl":"https://allison-daminger.squarespace.com/research","sourceLocator":"Research page, first paragraph after “The Cognitive Dimension of Household Labor” (page lines 11–12); the second paragraph and listed 2019/2020 papers are used only to confirm project navigation","boundary":"This is the author’s project introduction, not a complete paper or individual interview read in full for this lesson. The website’s 170+ interviews are not treated as the 2019 paper’s sample size, nor used to claim universal causation or reduced burdens in any particular family.","href":"https://allison-daminger.squarespace.com/research"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.09","start_line":4834,"end_line":4849,"startLine":4834,"endLine":4849}],"outputId":"section-d-09","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Determine whether the topic is experience, relationships, care work, recognized standing, or specific structural outcomes.","Check voluntariness, fairness, capacity, and effects separately; costs cannot replace these judgments.","Distinguish objects and clocks in death, separation, relationship endings, and organizational exit, preserving the boundary between hope and factual prediction.","Check noticing, arranging, reminding, verifying, and being on call beyond execution, acknowledging one act of help and long-term burden reduction separately.","For continuity after exit, retain both safety and independently grounded bounded obligations; multidimensional evaluation must not override the person’s life goals."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: shopping help is real, while coordination has not transferred","text":"Suppose A prepares the entire list, checks stock, reminds B to leave, and checks omissions, while B completes one shopping trip according to the list. B has actually taken up purchasing execution, so it cannot be called no help at all; if A still does all noticing, arranging, and checking, this alone cannot establish fair sharing of A’s long-term care burden.","assumptions":"The people and tasks are teaching constructions by the explanatory author, not published interviews.","boundary":"No actual work hours or psychological burdens were measured; one example cannot establish long-term personality or the full picture of fairness."},{"title":"Expanding the original teaching example: exit and safe continuity","text":"Suppose that after a relationship ends, direct contact must stop to protect one person’s safety, while a dependent still needs continuity of care. Analysis should separately check safety needs, independent obligations, and feasible continuity arrangements; the dependent’s needs cannot require the victim to remain exposed, nor can justified exit erase every obligation still needing examination.","assumptions":"Constructed following the original; no real child, family, or judicial decision is involved.","boundary":"Does not determine who legally owes which obligations or appoint an actual successor to care; real high-risk situations should be handled through corresponding professional and safety procedures."},{"title":"Teaching construction: handling errands cannot replace a loved person","text":"Imagine that after someone loses a close companion, friends take over weekly shopping and deliveries, genuinely reducing practical tasks. The person's continuing intense sense of loss does not establish that the friends' help is ineffective, nor mean mourning should end because the errands are handled. Work burdens, relational meaning, and emotional processes should be described separately.","assumptions":"An entirely fictional situation of loss and support, not a clinical bereavement case.","boundary":"No mourning deadline is set, no illness inferred, and no particular emotional outcome required."},{"title":"Teaching construction: an action remains open to evaluation despite unmet expectations","text":"Imagine someone voluntarily arranges quiet companionship for a neighbor, but that day the neighbor prefers solitude and the visit does not bring the expected comfort. The intention to help, actual arrangements, and mismatch with the response can be described, then support adjusted. The unrealized effect must not be falsely recorded as success, but neither must the action be said never to have occurred or to be wholly beyond evaluation.","assumptions":"People and reactions are teaching assumptions.","boundary":"Intentions do not exempt one from listening to responses, and disappointing outcomes do not determine personality."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"He wants to do it and contributes a great deal, so care is fairly distributed and refusal is safe.","effect":"Willingness, cost, fairness, and refusal conditions need separate checks; they cannot replace one another.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Someone has taken over all the tasks, so the person should no longer feel loss.","effect":"Task succession is not replacement of the person in the relationship and does not control the duration of mourning.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Companionship produced no detectable group-level structural change, so it has no meaning.","effect":"An evidence threshold for a specific structural outcome is not an admission requirement for every value in life.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why cannot long-term burden reduction be assessed only by counting task execution?","answer":"Recognizing needs, arranging, reminding, checking, and remaining on standby may still fall to the same person, while managing help can itself add work.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does low-cost help necessarily lack goodwill?","answer":"No. Cost is not a universal measure of goodwill or love; judge actual relational conduct and responses.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why should hope and probability judgments be separated?","answer":"Valuing a future provides no evidence of its probability; plans and commitments separately involve capabilities and arrangements.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does someone taking over tasks not guarantee the disappearance of loss?","answer":"A person in a relationship differs from a work function; reducing errands does not replace a loved person.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-09/figures/section-d-09.png","alt":"Love/care/recognition: experience, relations, and norms are not compressed into a single function; voluntariness/fairness and capability/effects require separate checks; beyond task execution: noticing, arranging, reminding, checking, and standby; one instance of help may be real, while long-term burden reduction requires further evidence; safe continuity after exit: no forced continuing exposure, with independent bounded duties checked separately; tasks may be replaceable without relationships being replaceable, and meaning need not first receive an overall score. Drawn by the author based on D.09; people and relationships are teaching constructions, not judgments about duties or stages of mourning in actual cases.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on D.09; people and relationships are teaching constructions, not judgments about duties or stages of mourning in actual cases."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selection, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Switch whether both execution and coordination have been taken up, refusal is safe, and independent continuity duties exist to check bounded conclusions.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Limited to the researcher's publicly stated research object as an entry point for examining coordination labor. Factual depth stops at the original page read; no comprehensive review of the research is claimed."},"openQuestions":["The original research for all entries 09.01–09.09 was not obtained; the full arguments or reception histories of the intellectual traditions cannot be reconstructed.","The complete paper and original interviews for this case were not obtained. The researcher's introduction supports only the concept's and project's existence, not individual cases or population proportions."],"taskId":"S-D-09","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 4657–4672; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["D.09: terminology, contract, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"daminger-project","title":"Research — The Cognitive Dimension of Household Labor","url":"https://allison-daminger.squarespace.com/research","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Page as accessed, with no publication date shown","locator":"First paragraph after the matching project heading (lines 11–12); the adjacent second paragraph and 2019/2020 paper links serve only as navigation","claim_support":["The researcher includes anticipating needs, identifying options, deciding, and monitoring in the study of cognitive labor"],"limits":["A project introduction, not the full text or original interviews; it does not support inferences about individual cases, the 2019 sample size, or general effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"D.09　爱、照护、承认、意义与生活质量; Chinese source lines 5108–5123; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-09/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-09/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-09/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-09/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"eb319a66655822d0c85b78777171e912482d830e2152ae00c6f5f3522191426e","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-09.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-09.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-09.md"};function d(h,e,u,p,g,f){const n=t("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s("",1)),r(n,{section:"D.09"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(l,[["render",d]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
